@page "/proof"

<PageTitle>Measured, not assumed · louis-agent</PageTitle>

<section class="band">
    <span class="kicker">Measured, not assumed</span>
    <h2 class="chrome">Every number here comes from the ledger or an eval run.</h2>
    <div class="grid-3">
        <div class="panel stat">
            <span class="stat-value chrome">$0.66 → $0.13</span>
            <span class="stat-note">a 10-fix benchmark run before and after prompt caching, on Claude Haiku. All 10 fixes still confirmed, input cost −87%. About R10.91 → R2.15.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="panel stat">
            <span class="stat-value chrome">72 / 72</span>
            <span class="stat-note">Puls's routing eval on the first baseline, and the cook 23 of 24. $0.37 for both, live. About R6.12.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="panel stat">
            <span class="stat-value chrome">$0</span>
            <span class="stat-note">to replay an eval run: it records the model's answers and the pages it read, so it re-scores for nothing</span>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="band-note">The rand are at R16.5331 to the dollar, the ECB's reference rate of 2026-10-09 in the repository's rates file. The apps show the live rate.</p>
</section>

<section class="band">
    <span class="kicker">How the rand come about</span>
    <CardDeck Columns="2">
        <Card Title="Priced in dollars, once" Summary="Every model request is one record in the ledger, priced by a dated price table, in the currency that was charged.">
            <p>Every model request lands in the usage ledger as one record with its four kinds of token (in, cache write, cached, out), priced by a dated price table for that model on that day. A price the table doesn't hold stays unknown, never 0. The ledger is the only source of cost, and it keeps dollars, because that is what was charged.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card Title="Rand for display, with the rate's date" Summary="A screen converts with the European Central Bank's rates and says which day's rate it used.">
            <p>A screen turns dollars into rand with a rates file: how much of each currency one dollar buys, from the European Central Bank's reference rates, with the date it was read. The live rate is fetched when it can be. The file in the repository is the fallback, so a container offline still converts. Puls shows every answer's cost this way, in rand, and the ledger underneath never changes.</p>
        </Card>
    </CardDeck>
</section>

<section class="band">
    <span class="kicker">How it is checked</span>
    <div class="grid-2">
        <article class="panel">
            <h3>Code graders first</h3>
            <p>Fixed rules are free and give the same score every time. A model judge comes in only where code can't decide, and only after it agrees with human labels: 35 of 36 on the cook's labels before it was trusted.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="panel">
            <h3>pass@k and pass^k</h3>
            <p>Run a task k times: did it pass at least once, and did it pass every time? An agent you sell needs the second.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="panel">
            <h3>One harness, every check</h3>
            <p>Every check, regression and demo is a C# eval on one harness, kept beside the identity or pack it proves. An eval is a folder of files: the samples, the rubric, the checks.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="panel">
            <h3>Cost from the ledger, never a grader</h3>
            <p>A run's cost is the sum of its priced ledger records. A turn's line keeps the four kinds of token apart (in, cache write, cached, out), since each is priced differently.</p>
        </article>
    </div>
</section>
